Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<TEntryPoint>`** to host your server in-memory, then connect the MCP C# SDK client to it via the factory's `HttpClient`.

### Pattern

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using ModelContextProtocol.Client;
using ModelContextProtocol.Transport;

public class McpIntegrationTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly WebApplicationFactory<Program> _factory;

    public McpIntegrationTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
        => _factory = factory;

    [Fact]
    public async Task ListTools_ReturnsExpectedTools()
    {
        // The factory gives you an HttpClient wired to the in-memory TestServer
        var httpClient = _factory.CreateClient();

        // Point the MCP SDK's transport at the test client
        var transport = new SseClientTransport(
            new SseClientTransportOptions
            {
                Endpoint = new Uri(httpClient.BaseAddress!, "/sse"),
                // or "/mcp" for Streamable HTTP
            },
            httpClient);           // ← key: pass the factory's HttpClient

        await using var client = await McpClientFactory.CreateAsync(transport);

        var tools = await client.ListToolsAsync();

        Assert.NotEmpty(tools);
        Assert.Contains(tools, t => t.Name == "my-tool");
    }
}
```

### Why this works

| Piece | Role |
|---|---|
| `WebApplicationFactory` | Boots your full ASP.NET pipeline (middleware, DI, routing) in-process with no open port |
| `CreateClient()` | Returns an `HttpClient` whose handler routes requests to the in-memory `TestServer` |
| `SseClientTransport` / `StreamableHttpClientTransport` | Accepts an external `HttpClient`, so it goes through the test pipeline instead of the network |

### Tips

1. **Streamable HTTP** — if your server uses the newer streamable HTTP transport, swap in `StreamableHttpClientTransport` with the same `httpClient`.
2. **Override services** — use `_factory.WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))` to stub dependencies (databases, APIs).
3. **Call tools** — `await client.CallToolAsync("my-tool", new { input = "value" })` exercises the full request→route→handler→response cycle.
4. **Make `Program` visible** — add `[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo("YourTests")]` or a `public partial class Program { }` in your server project.

This gives you true end-to-end MCP protocol coverage (JSON-RPC framing, SSE streaming, content negotiation) with the speed of an in-process call.
